All right. So we've been talking a lot about connectivity. Jensen, I just covered this.

42:34.460 --> 42:42.140
So let's dive in now, right? Let's go one level deeper. So AI infrastructure spans every distance.

42:42.140 --> 42:48.380
It spans from hundreds or even 1,000 kilometers between data centers to just millimeters inside

42:48.380 --> 42:53.820
the package. Every one of those distances, it requires a different solution. It's a different

42:53.820 --> 42:58.460
technology, different engineering team. It's a completely different set of experts.

42:58.460 --> 43:04.380
And in many cases, it's a different supply chain. So these are not variations of the same problem.

43:04.860 --> 43:08.540
What you have here is fundamentally different engineering challenges. And that's what we're

43:08.540 --> 43:14.220
going to walk through next. All right, so let's start with the longest distance. Jensen referred

43:14.220 --> 43:20.540
to this. This is scale across connecting data centers together. Now, every major cloud provider

43:20.540 --> 43:25.100
has hundreds of data centers around the world. And all those data centers need to communicate

43:25.100 --> 43:30.780
with each other. This is fundamentally a long distance connectivity problem. We're talking

43:30.780 --> 43:36.140
about lengths that can span hundreds or even 1,000 kilometers. This requires very specific,

43:36.140 --> 43:42.860
very complex technology called coherent modulation. At the heart of it is a specialized digital signal

43:42.860 --> 43:49.660
processor or DSP. It's designed to push enormous amounts of data, cross fiber optic cables over

43:49.660 --> 43:56.220
very long distances with extremely high reliability. There's only a few companies in the world that

43:56.220 --> 44:01.820
build these coherent DSPs. And we're one of them. Marvell has been a leader in this technology

44:01.820 --> 44:07.900
for many generations. We build optical modules that contain all the electronics needed to drive

44:07.900 --> 44:17.580
and modulate the laser and transmit data over long distances. So I've got a little show and tell here

44:18.140 --> 44:21.980
in my pocket. I'm not holding up a chip this time. I'm holding up an optical module.

44:21.980 --> 44:28.700
This is one of our coherent optical modules. This is an incredibly complex piece of engineering.

44:30.140 --> 44:35.740
At Marvell, we build the entire module. This is ours. It includes the advanced node CMOS DSP.

44:36.620 --> 44:41.260
It's among the most complex chips, just the DSP alone that we design at Marvell.

44:41.260 --> 44:47.340
But it also incorporates inside our fourth generation silicon photonics technology.

44:48.060 --> 44:53.900
That's inside here. We've been developing that technology in production for a decade

44:53.900 --> 45:00.300
on silicon photonics. It also includes our own broadband analog components that we designed,

45:00.300 --> 45:04.860
which is designed in silicon germanium. So Marvell pioneered this technology,

45:04.860 --> 45:11.180
starting with 100 gigabits per second a decade ago, then moving to 400 gig and now

45:11.180 --> 45:17.260
shipping 800 gig in volume. And later this year, we'll be sampling the world's first

45:17.260 --> 45:23.660
1.6 terabit 2 nanometer coherent optical solution. And that couldn't come in a better time.

45:23.660 --> 45:30.140
Demand for bandwidth has never been greater. All right, now let's go inside the data center.

45:30.860 --> 45:35.340
So these data centers can be very large, spanning hundreds of meters, and they contain racks

45:35.340 --> 45:40.140
and racks of compute servers. Now, each rack typically has a switch at the top

45:40.220 --> 45:44.860
with servers connected into that switch. Those rack level switches connect to the spine,

45:44.860 --> 45:49.660
and then the core switches. This creates the network fabric that ties the entire data center

45:49.660 --> 45:56.140
together. And all of that is connected through fiber optic cables. Now once again, optical modules

45:56.140 --> 46:02.140
drive data transmission over those fiber optic cables. But this time the modulation scheme is

46:02.140 --> 46:08.540
different. Instead of coherent technology, we use a more power optimized modulation technology,

46:08.620 --> 46:14.460
which is called PAM4. So the two key semiconductor solutions for this part of the market are the

46:14.460 --> 46:20.780
PAM4 chip set inside the module, and then the cloud switching infrastructure that ties the data center

46:20.780 --> 46:28.460
together. Marvell builds both. Starting with the PAM4 chip set, we build the industry's leading

46:28.460 --> 46:35.900
PAM4 DSP solution. And also the high speed analog components that go around them, including

46:35.900 --> 46:40.540
transimpedance amplifiers, or TIAs, and laser drivers. These are also in Silicon Germanium,

46:40.540 --> 46:45.820
by the way. And we've led the industry through every major transition of PAM technology, starting

46:45.820 --> 46:56.140
at 50 gig, 100 gig, 200, 400, and 800. Then last year, we began ramping Marvell's 1.6T 3nm PAM4

46:56.140 --> 47:02.540
solutions, leading the industry's transition to 1.6T connectivity. Now for ethernet switching,

47:02.540 --> 47:08.620
Marvell has a similarly complete portfolio of products from 12.8 terabits to 51.2 terabits.

47:09.180 --> 47:17.420
And today, we announced our new 100T ethernet switch, specifically designed for AI data centers

47:17.420 --> 47:26.940
with the industry's lowest power. Woo! Special announcement for Computex. We waited. So you

47:26.940 --> 47:31.340
put it all together, we provide a complete solution for connectivity inside the data center.

47:33.260 --> 47:38.700
Now let's move inside the rack. The goal here is to connect the largest possible number of processors

47:38.700 --> 47:44.780
together in a full any-to-any configuration. In other words, every processor can communicate

47:44.780 --> 47:49.740
directly with every other processor. And Jensen talked about this. The first company to bring

47:49.740 --> 47:56.860
this architecture to market was NVIDIA with NVL72, named for the 72 GPUs connected together inside

47:56.860 --> 48:01.660
a single rack. And this required a completely different approach to connectivity. It was a

48:01.660 --> 48:06.620
different class of switch and the ability to drive very high-speed signals over copper-backed

48:06.620 --> 48:14.060
planes inside the rack. So today, this is not the domain of optics. This is the domain of copper.

48:14.860 --> 48:19.580
And the core differentiator here is the electrical CERDES technology, not the optical.

48:20.220 --> 48:27.260
Now Marvell also has leading electrical CERDES at 200 gigabits per second today. And we've

48:27.260 --> 48:31.980
demonstrated already over the last couple of years 400 gigabits per second for the future.

48:32.860 --> 48:38.220
So we're building this CERDES technology into our customers' custom silicon and their XPUs

48:38.220 --> 48:44.540
and also into our own scale-up switches. All right, now let's go all the way inside the package.

48:46.140 --> 48:50.140
Here we're not talking about meters anymore. We're talking about millimeters. And you might

48:50.140 --> 48:55.100
not actually think about this as a connectivity challenge. But today, most advanced chips have

48:55.180 --> 49:01.500
multiple chiplets inside the package. So when you have 2.5D or 3D packaging, it's fundamentally a

49:01.500 --> 49:06.460
connectivity technology, actually. And it allows these chiplets to sit very close together inside

49:06.460 --> 49:12.620
a package and communicate through ultra-high-speed, short-reach, die-to-die interfaces. And Marvell

49:12.620 --> 49:17.660
has leading die-to-die CERDES and leading capability in advanced packaging, allowing our

49:17.660 --> 49:22.540
customers to build some of the most complex, unique, multi-die chips in the industry.

49:22.540 --> 49:30.380
So as you can see, connectivity for AI data centers requires a very broad portfolio of

49:30.380 --> 49:35.980
technologies. Each distance requires a very different solution. And Marvell has the industry's

49:35.980 --> 49:41.980
most complete portfolio, from millimeters to kilometers. Every hop, every distance. And it

49:41.980 --> 49:48.540
turns out having all of those capabilities under one roof is unusual. It's unique. When we go and

49:48.540 --> 49:53.100
compete, normally there's a different set of companies that we compete against in each one

49:53.100 --> 49:58.140
of these categories across these different distances. But this is what makes us unique.

49:58.140 --> 50:04.620
We're the one-stop shop. We're the leader across the entire connectivity stack. And that brings us to

50:04.620 --> 50:11.260
the next major challenge facing the industry. So what you probably notice as I describe these

50:11.260 --> 50:16.140
different solutions in the last couple of slides is there's different solutions for different

50:16.140 --> 50:20.860
distances and that some of those connections today are optical and some of those connections today

50:20.860 --> 50:27.180
are electrical. And it's actually defined by distance. And so the connections on the left side

50:27.180 --> 50:32.860
of this chart are optical today. That means they use fiber optic cables to transmit light with

50:32.860 --> 50:37.900
complex electronics on either side of the cable to drive and modulate the laser that's transmitting

50:37.900 --> 50:43.420
that light. Connections on the right side of this are electrical. So they use copper cables,

50:43.420 --> 50:48.220
or just copper traces that are printed on the circuit board, or even microscopic copper routing

50:48.220 --> 50:54.060
inside the package. So the common theme here is copper. And in the middle, you see the wall,

50:55.180 --> 51:00.060
the copper wall. And the wall is defined by the longest distance you can transmit a signal over

51:00.060 --> 51:05.580
copper. So before you have to move to an optical, before you have to move to an optical connection.

51:05.580 --> 51:10.700
So this is an important distinction because copper is simple and it's low cost. And as Jensen said,

51:10.700 --> 51:16.540
you want to use it for as long as you can. It's very practical. But optics and optics is more

51:16.540 --> 51:21.820
complicated. It requires lasers, photonics, complex electronics. So it's a bigger lift,

51:21.820 --> 51:26.140
but it's going to be needed. And the copper wall, what I'm here to tell you today is it's about to

51:26.140 --> 51:31.660
move. It's going to move again. And it's going to take over the rack itself. So this is creating

51:31.660 --> 51:37.580
an explosion in demand for the optical industry. And incredibly complex engineering challenges

51:37.580 --> 51:43.980
are coming along the way. So why is this happening? So it's not just somebody's preference to go do

51:43.980 --> 51:50.780
this. This is physics. The distance a signal can travel over a copper cable is inversely proportional

51:50.780 --> 51:55.180
to the bandwidth. So every time you double the bandwidth, you have to cut the distance in half.

51:56.140 --> 52:00.220
Today, the highest speed production systems in the world run at 200 gigabits per second per

52:00.220 --> 52:04.780
lane, just to give you an example. So at that bandwidth, the cable length is limited to roughly

52:04.780 --> 52:10.060
two and a half meters. Now, by comparison, systems running at 100 gig could use about five meter

52:10.060 --> 52:14.700
cables. And the height of the rack is about two meters. So once you account for all the routing

52:14.700 --> 52:20.700
inside the rack, two and a half meters is right at the limit. So when we move to 400 gig, we can no

52:20.700 --> 52:27.180
longer fully connect the rack with copper. So the wall is moving. And it's moving now.

52:29.020 --> 52:32.940
Going forward, even the connections within the rack will become optical. And the whole industry

52:32.940 --> 52:37.420
knows this is coming. So we've been preparing for this moment, not just Marvell, but the industry.

52:37.420 --> 52:41.580
And you see this in Taiwan, by the way, and the supply chain and the ramp up that's happening.

52:41.580 --> 52:47.660
The ramifications for this are actually enormous, because each time the wall moves one step to the

52:47.660 --> 52:54.460
right, the number of connections that you have goes up by at least an order of magnitude. So it's

52:54.460 --> 53:00.060
creating this explosion in demand, as I mentioned, and the optical supply chain needs to scale up

53:00.140 --> 53:05.420
massively and be ready. But we've seen this movie before. Okay, I mean, 20 years ago, and I remember

53:05.420 --> 53:11.180
this when state of the art was 10 gigabits per second inside the data center, 10 gig. And we

53:11.180 --> 53:15.740
use copper cables all across the data center. Optics back then was reserved for just very,

53:15.740 --> 53:21.260
very long distances. It was essentially like a telecom technology. But when the wall moved,

53:21.260 --> 53:26.380
the optics industry actually rose to the challenge. And today, all the hyperscale data centers in the

53:26.380 --> 53:30.940
world, they're all optically connected. And as we saw in that transition, it did require new

53:30.940 --> 53:36.540
solutions. You couldn't use the same power hungry kind of telecom approach, which is where Pam four

53:36.540 --> 53:41.980
came in. It's optimized for power, density and reach, and requirements specifically tuned to

53:41.980 --> 53:47.100
inside the data center. And Marvell was one of the key innovators there. So we're about to see

53:47.100 --> 53:53.580
the same wave of innovation needed as optics moves inside the rack. And that's what the technology

53:53.580 --> 53:58.860
called co package optics or CPO, you hear a lot about this now, I'm going to tell you more.

54:00.140 --> 54:05.420
CPO is a technology where we bring the optical connections all the way to the package itself,

54:06.060 --> 54:11.820
right next to the compute, either the custom compute, or the switching silicon. And the fundamental

54:11.820 --> 54:18.060
challenge we're solving with CPO is density and power. Now remember, the number of connections

54:18.060 --> 54:24.540
inside the rack is like 10x, the number of connections between the racks. So we try it.

54:24.540 --> 54:29.580
So if you just try to use the same optical technology used across the racks in the data center,

54:29.580 --> 54:34.460
you wouldn't have enough power, you wouldn't have enough physical space, you cannot fit all these

54:34.460 --> 54:39.420
standard optical modules and cables as they are today, it just doesn't work. It's not possible.

54:39.420 --> 54:44.300
So the industry has been inventing this co package optics concept, which brings the optical fiber

54:44.300 --> 54:49.020
right to the package, it tightly couples the electronics that drive the signal over the fiber

54:49.020 --> 54:54.540
directly with the custom compute or switching silicon. So this is a massive change. And

54:55.500 --> 54:59.740
what's hard because you're combining some of the most advanced technologies in the chip industry,

55:00.300 --> 55:05.900
leading edge CMOS, silicon photonics, advanced packaging, optical interconnect,

55:05.900 --> 55:11.740
all manufactured in this in a small tightly integrated system. So complexity is very high,

55:11.740 --> 55:15.980
but it's the only way to continue scaling bandwidth and overcome this limitation that I

55:15.980 --> 55:21.580
talked about with copper while reducing power at the same time. So this is where the industry's

55:21.580 --> 55:26.700
headed. And this is one of the reasons that Marvell has invested for more than a decade

55:26.700 --> 55:32.300
in silicon photonics, optical DSPs, all the analog broadband components around it,

55:32.300 --> 55:37.340
and all the advanced packaging, you need to pull this off. It needs to all come together actually

55:37.340 --> 55:43.900
in CPO. So this isn't some futuristic thing, guys. Okay, it's happening now. And in fact,

55:43.900 --> 55:49.900
I brought a couple of Marvell examples with me today. So let's do a quick, a quick show and tell.

55:49.900 --> 55:57.580
Okay. Okay, so over here, you have a traditional ethernet switch. This is our 100 t tera link

55:57.580 --> 56:01.260
switch that we announced today. And you guys are the first to see it actually, everybody here in

56:01.260 --> 56:07.340
the room. You can see the switch in the middle of the board. Copper traces inside the PCB carry

56:07.340 --> 56:11.100
the signal to the front panel, which is here. And this is where all the optical modules plug in.

56:11.820 --> 56:18.620
Now let's move over here. This is a CPO based switch right here. Now notice that there's still

56:18.620 --> 56:23.020
the switch silicon in the middle. That's right in the center of the die of the package. In this case,

56:23.020 --> 56:32.380
this is our 51.2 t switch. And all around the edges are 16 3.2 t optical engines. So the 16

56:32.380 --> 56:39.260
times 3.2, you get 51.2. So this is, this is the fibers directly attached now to the, to these

56:39.260 --> 56:43.900
engines. It's not to the front panel. So we've completely eliminated the copper traces on the

56:43.900 --> 56:49.420
PCB light comes directly out of the package. Okay, this is a very, very complex, complex piece of

56:49.420 --> 56:54.540
engineering. And it was very, it was very cool to be able to show this off today. Okay, so

56:55.340 --> 56:59.740
co package optics is here and the industry is scaling up to meet the challenge. And as we've

56:59.740 --> 57:04.860
seen time and time again, every time we reach a physical barrier, we break through it with technology

57:04.860 --> 57:10.780
and innovation. In this case, by replacing copper with fiber, because unlike electrons traveling

57:10.780 --> 57:16.220
over copper wires, the distance that photons can carry a signal through glass is largely unrelated

57:16.220 --> 57:21.020
to the bandwidth. So as AI infrastructure demands even higher transmission speeds and needs to scale

57:21.020 --> 57:25.900
to larger and more complex systems, spanning millions of processors woven together now, not

57:25.900 --> 57:31.740
thousands or hundreds, optical connectivity will increasingly become the de facto solution. So

57:31.740 --> 57:36.620
the real question becomes, what does it take to deliver optics across the full AI infrastructure

57:36.620 --> 57:43.740
stack? What's it going to take? Well, it starts with recognizing there is no single technology

57:43.740 --> 57:48.780
for the entire data center. It's not how this works. There's no one size fits all, there's no

57:48.780 --> 57:55.100
one size fits all solution. There's no shortcuts. There's no easy way to the end here. There's not

57:55.100 --> 58:00.620
a single architecture modulation scheme, frequency band, you know, unique technology that's going

58:00.620 --> 58:07.660
to do it all. There's no free lunch. That's why we are pursuing a bunch of different unique

58:07.660 --> 58:12.860
optical paths across every distance to get here. Each one of these technologies that I have up

58:12.860 --> 58:18.460
here is optimized for a different design point. Each one enables a critical part of the infrastructure

58:18.460 --> 58:23.740
and addressing different requirements for density, bandwidth, and power and integration

58:23.740 --> 58:29.500
all across the stack. So if optical interconnect is the underlying technology for which next

58:29.500 --> 58:34.300
generation AI infrastructure is built, then Marvell is building the broadest portfolio with the

