01:06:29.820 --> 01:06:37.580
Okay. So like we said, the future of AI data centers is all optically connected infrastructure.

01:06:38.140 --> 01:06:41.740
And you heard him say it, right? This is going to drive a tidal wave of growth,

01:06:41.740 --> 01:06:48.380
innovation that's needed in scale and manufacturing. But what does that inevitable future actually

01:06:48.380 --> 01:06:51.820
look like? I mean, if you just take a step back for a minute and you actually don't think about

01:06:51.820 --> 01:06:57.180
right now, think about 10 years in the future. And it's a world where a lot of the copper connections

01:06:57.180 --> 01:07:02.540
are gone. And just think about a world where data transmission now at some point is all optical.

01:07:02.780 --> 01:07:08.860
This is a world where then distance doesn't matter, actually. And that's a profound change.

01:07:09.900 --> 01:07:14.940
Servers, racks and overall data center architectures today have all been designed around the

01:07:14.940 --> 01:07:20.220
constraints of distance. And software workloads actually have also been optimized around those

01:07:20.220 --> 01:07:26.460
same constraints. But what if distance no longer matters? How might the architecture itself change

01:07:26.460 --> 01:07:31.420
and what new capabilities become possible when the infrastructure is no longer constrained by

01:07:31.420 --> 01:07:36.860
distance? So let's start with the scale up network in the rack. As we discussed earlier,

01:07:36.860 --> 01:07:41.580
this is where we can connect the largest possible number of processors together in a full any to

01:07:41.580 --> 01:07:46.300
any configuration. Now in the past, the size of this domain was limited by the length of the

01:07:46.300 --> 01:07:51.340
copper connection, but with optics distance doesn't matter. So now we can change the size of the

01:07:51.340 --> 01:07:59.740
scale up domain from 72 or 144 xpus or GPUs to 1000 or more, all optically interconnected.

01:08:00.380 --> 01:08:06.060
The implications for workloads are enormous. Today AI workloads must be broken down into

01:08:06.060 --> 01:08:11.500
smaller sub problems that fit within the scale up cluster, because communicating outside the

01:08:11.500 --> 01:08:17.820
cluster today is slower, much lower bandwidth. But optically interconnected systems can manage

01:08:17.820 --> 01:08:21.980
workloads on an order of magnitude larger. And it does not stop there, by the way.

01:08:23.260 --> 01:08:26.380
What happens when the optical connectivity comes inside the server itself?

01:08:27.180 --> 01:08:33.100
Modern AI servers are composed of a certain number of CPUs, xpus memory and network interfaces.

01:08:33.100 --> 01:08:35.820
And the reason they're all in the same system is because of distance.

01:08:37.500 --> 01:08:42.140
CPUs and xpus need to access memory at very, very high bandwidth, which means they need to sit

01:08:42.140 --> 01:08:46.540
right next to each other on the board with copper traces serving as the connections between them.

01:08:47.180 --> 01:08:51.820
But in a future where these connections are all optical, distance actually doesn't matter.

01:08:52.460 --> 01:08:55.100
You can imagine a completely disaggregated architecture.

01:08:55.900 --> 01:09:04.060
xpus in one system, memory in another, agentic CPUs in another, which unlocks another possibility.

01:09:04.780 --> 01:09:12.380
In today's systems, the ratio of CPU and xpu or GPU, it's fixed. So these ratios have to be defined

01:09:12.380 --> 01:09:17.660
at the time the system is built and deployed. But no two workloads require exactly the same

01:09:17.660 --> 01:09:22.380
ratio. Jensen talked about this, actually, which means at any given time, some portion of the

01:09:22.380 --> 01:09:27.900
computer memory could be underutilized for a given workload. That costs money. But once we

01:09:27.900 --> 01:09:32.860
decompose the system into separate pools of compute and memory, and they're all optically

01:09:32.860 --> 01:09:38.620
interconnected, we can then decompose dedicated systems on the fly, which are then optimized for

01:09:38.620 --> 01:09:47.580
whatever the workload is. So imagine future data centers. A globally optically interconnected

01:09:47.660 --> 01:09:53.020
data infrastructure. These rigid boundaries we have today in the systems we have,

01:09:53.020 --> 01:09:59.820
they begin to disappear. Compute can now be pulled. Memory can be pulled. An infrastructure can be

01:09:59.820 --> 01:10:06.300
composed dynamically at scale. For the first time, architects can begin designing AI systems

01:10:06.300 --> 01:10:11.500
around the needs of the model, not around the limits of the interconnect.

01:10:11.580 --> 01:10:17.420
So this is where AI infrastructure is headed. It's a data center without distance, where compute,

01:10:17.420 --> 01:10:24.540
memory, networking, and photonics operate as one unified system. Where millions of resources

01:10:24.540 --> 01:10:30.140
across the data center can work together as if they were one machine. An architecture defined

01:10:30.140 --> 01:10:36.860
by the needs of the workload, not the limits of the connectivity. We believe this is the next

01:10:36.940 --> 01:10:42.460
era of computing infrastructure, and Marvell is helping build the connectivity foundation

01:10:42.460 --> 01:10:47.020
that will make all this possible. Thank you very much for your time today.

